const P = 'https://media.huanyuchen.com/misc/la-outdoors/';
---
<BaseLayout title="Trails, Canyons and Lakes Around Los Angeles — Huanyu Chen"
  description="Nineteen outings around Los Angeles, 2022–2025: ridges and a stone labyrinth in the Santa Monica Mountains, Switzer and Millard Falls, sunrise and snow on the Angeles Crest, San Gabriel Canyon, Runyon Canyon twice, Echo Park Lake, the Arboretum's peacocks, and walks with Wangcai at Machado Lake and in the Puente Hills.">
  <p class="muted"><a href="/misc">← Misc</a> · 2022–2025</p>
  <h1>Trails, Canyons and Lakes Around Los Angeles</h1>
  <p class="muted trip-sub">Short outings in the mountains, hills and parks of Los Angeles, 2022–2025.</p>

  <div class="callout trip-stats">
    <strong>19 outings · February 2022 – October 2025</strong><br />
    Santa Monica Mountains: Tuna Canyon, Corral Canyon (twice), Will Rogers, the Nancy Hoover Pohl Overlook ·
    San Gabriels: Switzer Falls, Millard Falls and Millard Ridge, the Angeles Crest at dawn, San Gabriel Canyon ·
    City hills: Runyon Canyon (twice), Griffith Park, Elysian Park ·
    Lakes and parks: the Arboretum, Silver Lake, Echo Park Lake, Machado Lake, the Puente Hills<br />
    One stone labyrinth · one kitten on a boulder · one peacock ·
    and, from 2025, Wangcai
  </div>

  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'map-la-outdoors.webp'}><img src={P + 'map-la-outdoors.webp'} alt="Map of the Los Angeles area with photo thumbnails at Tuna Canyon, Corral Canyon and Will Rogers in the Santa Monica Mountains, Switzer Falls, Millard Falls and two Angeles Crest stops in the San Gabriels, San Gabriel Canyon, Runyon Canyon, Griffith Park, Elysian Park, Silver Lake, Echo Park Lake, the Arboretum, Machado Lake and the Puente Hills." /></a>
    <figcaption>Nineteen outings, 2022–2025. Each place is marked where its photos were taken.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p>
    Most posts on this page are about trips. This one is about the opposite: the outings that fit into a morning or an
    afternoon, without leaving Los Angeles. Los Angeles is ringed by mountains. The Santa Monica Mountains run along
    the coast to the west, the San Gabriels stand behind the city to the north, and between them there are hills and
    parks inside the city itself. Over four years the camera roll picked up a lot of these short outings: a few
    hikes in 2022, a sunrise drive in 2023, and from 2024 on, more walks than hikes, eventually with
    <a href="/misc/wangcai-2025">Wangcai</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    Everything below is reconstructed from photos: what was photographed, where, and at what minute. The 2022
    outings are from my own camera roll; some of those hikes were with friends, who are not shown. From late 2023 on,
    many of the photos are my wife's, and those outings were ones we did together. A few places that are already on
    this site are only linked here: Griffith Observatory is in <a href="/misc/views-from-above">Views from Up High</a>,
    and the night on the Angeles Crest Highway is in <a href="/misc/stars#angeles-crest">the stars post</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>The Santa Monica Mountains</h2>
  <p>
    The first outing in the camera roll is <strong>Tuna Canyon Park</strong> above Malibu, on February 6, 2022, a
    hike with friends. The trail runs along a ridge of low green chaparral, and from it the ocean fills the whole
    horizon. At 10:53 the water was a flat, hazy blue, with the outline of an island far out on the left.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'tuna-ridge.webp'} alt="A green chaparral ridge in the foreground falling away to a hazy blue ocean, with a faint island on the horizon at left." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The ocean from the ridge at Tuna Canyon, 10:53 am.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Twenty minutes later the trail reached a flat dirt clearing with a <strong>labyrinth</strong> laid out in stones:
    a spiral of small rocks, several rings deep, with paths of bare dirt between them.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'tuna-labyrinth.webp'} alt="A spiral labyrinth of small stones laid out in rings on flat dry ground, with tufts of brush between the rings." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The stone labyrinth, 11:15 am.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Three weeks later, on February 26, I was on the <strong>Corral Canyon</strong> trail in Malibu by nine in the
    morning. The slope above the Pacific Coast Highway was covered in yellow wildflowers, with the beach and a row of
    beach houses below. Twenty minutes further in, the trail turned up the canyon, a green V of hillside with a
    strip of ocean at the bottom.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'corral-wildflowers.webp'} alt="A hillside of yellow wildflowers above a beach and a row of houses along the coast, with blue ocean beyond." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Wildflowers above the coast, 9:10 am.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'corral-canyon.webp'} alt="A green canyon with a dirt trail along its side, its slopes opening onto a strip of ocean under a light blue sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Up the canyon, 9:34 am.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    On March 6 it was <strong>Will Rogers State Historic Park</strong> in Pacific Palisades: up to Inspiration Point,
    then along the Backbone Trail. The point of that stretch, at 11:22, was a footbridge: a narrow wooden deck with
    white railings, crossing a gap in the ridge toward a hill of pale rock.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'will-rogers-bridge.webp'} alt="A narrow wooden footbridge with white metal railings crossing a gap in a ridge, leading toward a brushy hill of pale rock under a blue sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The footbridge on the Backbone Trail, 11:22 am.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    In June there was one more early start in these mountains, on June 18, to the
    <strong>Nancy Hoover Pohl Overlook</strong> in the Santa Monica Mountains above Beverly Hills. At 8:25 the sky was
    still gray, and the canyon below opened onto the flat city in the haze.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'hoover-overlook.webp'} alt="A green wooded canyon opening onto a wide hazy view of the city, under a gray cloudy sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>From the Nancy Hoover Pohl Overlook, 8:25 am.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Two years later, on March 3, 2024, we went back to Corral Canyon with friends, this time under a sky full of storm
    clouds. The hills had turned a deep green, and the clouds were breaking up over them in the late afternoon.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'corral-storm-hills.webp'} alt="Green hills under a sky of big white and gray clouds with patches of blue, with green brush in the foreground." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Corral Canyon under storm clouds, March 3, 2024, 3:54 pm.</figcaption></figure>

  <h2>The San Gabriels</h2>
  <p>
    The San Gabriel Mountains are the wall behind Pasadena and the San Gabriel Valley, and the Angeles Crest Highway
    climbs into them from La Cañada Flintridge. On February 13, 2022, a week after Tuna Canyon, I went up it with
    friends to hike to <strong>Switzer Falls</strong>. The first photo, at 10:03, looks out across ridge after ridge of
    the range; by 11:34 the trail was down in the canyon, following a creek over gray boulders under bare
    trees.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'switzer-ridges.webp'} alt="Layers of brown and green mountain ridges under a blue sky with thin clouds, above burned shrubs and dry brush." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The San Gabriels, 10:03 am.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'switzer-creek.webp'} alt="A shallow creek running over gray boulders beside a dirt trail, under tall bare trees and green undergrowth." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The creek below Switzer Falls, 11:34 am.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    That June there were two short morning hikes above Altadena. On June 11 it was <strong>Millard Falls</strong>, a
    thin stream of water sliding down a cleft of pale, smooth rock with green plants in the cracks. Two weeks later, on June 25,
    it was the ridge above it, with the whole valley spread out below.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'millard-falls.webp'} alt="A narrow waterfall streaking down a cleft of pale gray rock, with green plants growing in the cracks." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Millard Falls, June 11, 2022, 9:22 am.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'millard-ridge.webp'} alt="A wide view from a green brushy ridge over a valley of towns to the hazy horizon, under a clear blue sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>From Millard Ridge, June 25, 10:01 am.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h3>The Angeles Crest at dawn</h3>
  <p>
    The best morning in the San Gabriels was February 18, 2023, when my wife and I drove up the Angeles Crest
    Highway for the sunrise. The first photos were taken at 6:27 at a turnout on the highway, before the sun was up.
    At 6:33 the sky over the ridges to the east was bright orange, and by 6:36 the sun had cleared the
    mountains.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'crest-sunrise.webp'} alt="The sun rising over dark layered mountain ridges, with rays spreading out and an orange band of sky above the ridgeline." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Sunrise from the Angeles Crest Highway, 6:36 am.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    A few minutes later, at 6:41, the same view had pine branches hanging across the top of the frame. Then we drove
    on. By 7:24 we were farther east along the highway, high enough for snow: patches of it lay on the rocks between tall
    pines, in bright sunshine.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'crest-branches.webp'} alt="The rising sun over mountain ridges in an orange sky, framed by pine branches hanging across the top." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Through the pine branches, 6:41 am.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'crest-snow-pines.webp'} alt="Tall pines on a slope with patches of snow on the rocks between them, under a blue sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Snow between the pines, 7:24 am.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    The last stop going out, at 7:40, was a turnout looking out over the high country: a deep valley in shadow, and beyond it a
    row of bare gray peaks in full sun, with a few clouds sitting above them. Two minutes later, a little further
    along, a single pine stood on the slope against the blue.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'crest-high-country.webp'} alt="A shadowed valley with scattered pines in front of a row of sunlit gray mountain peaks under a blue sky with clouds." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The high country, 7:40 am.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'crest-pine.webp'} alt="A lone tall pine on a slope above a valley, with sunlit brown mountainsides and a blue sky behind." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A pine on the slope, 7:42 am.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    On the way down, at 8:38, we stopped once more where the road looks back over the front ridges, and downtown
    Los Angeles stood small in the haze far below.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'crest-downtown.webp'} alt="Downtown Los Angeles towers small on the horizon in a gray haze, seen over a dark ridge with power-line towers." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Downtown in the haze, 8:38 am.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>San Gabriel Canyon</h3>
  <p>
    On December 2, 2023, we drove up <strong>San Gabriel Canyon</strong> from Azusa, along Highway 39. At 2:33 the road
    ran above the reservoir behind <strong>Morris Dam</strong>, a long arm of green water between steep mountainsides,
    its banks showing a pale ring where the water had been higher.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'morris-reservoir.webp'} alt="A long reservoir of green water between steep mountain slopes, with a pale band of exposed rock along its banks." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The reservoir behind Morris Dam, 2:33 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Farther up the canyon, at 2:48, a red steel bridge carried the road across a wide, pale riverbed of stones. At
    3:13 we were down by the water, at a clear stream running between boulders below a rock wall.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'canyon-bridge.webp'} alt="A red steel truss bridge on concrete piers crossing a wide pale riverbed of stones, below a brushy canyon wall." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A bridge over the riverbed, 2:48 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'canyon-creek.webp'} alt="A clear stream running between boulders below a steep rock wall, with bare branches against a blue sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>By the water, 3:13 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    We stayed in the canyon until sunset. The last photos, at 5:00, show the ridges in silhouette against a pink and
    orange sky.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'canyon-sunset.webp'} alt="Dark canyon ridges in silhouette against a pink and orange sunset sky, with a faint snowy peak in the gap between them." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Sunset in San Gabriel Canyon, 5:00 pm.</figcaption></figure>

  <h2>Hills in the city</h2>
  <p>
    Not every hike in Los Angeles needs a drive into the mountains. <strong>Runyon Canyon</strong> is in Hollywood
    itself. On October 23, 2022, I went up at sunset. At 5:48 the sun was low over the
    basin, and downtown stood out as a cluster of towers in the orange haze.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'runyon-skyline-2022.webp'} alt="The downtown Los Angeles skyline as a small cluster of towers in orange haze at sunset, above dark hills and trees." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Downtown from Runyon Canyon, 5:48 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Near the top, at the lookout called Cloud's Rest, a gray tabby kitten was sitting on a boulder, with the sunset
    behind it.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'runyon-kitten.webp'} alt="A small gray tabby kitten sitting on a granite boulder, looking at the camera, with the city and an orange sunset sky behind." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The kitten at Cloud's Rest, 6:05 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    On September 7, 2024, we were in Hollywood in the afternoon. From the street, at 4:48, the
    <strong>Hollywood Sign</strong> showed above a row of palms. That evening we walked a trail in
    <strong>Griffith Park</strong>, east of the sign, and at 7:04 the sun set over the hills.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'hollywood-sign.webp'} alt="The Hollywood Sign on a brown hillside with a radio mast above it, seen over the tops of palm trees under a pale blue sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Hollywood Sign from the street, 4:48 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'griffith-sunset.webp'} alt="The sun setting as a bright orange sliver behind dark hills, under a sky fading from orange to gray-blue." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Sunset from a Griffith Park trail, 7:04 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Wangcai came home in January 2025, and after that the city hills became dog walks. On February 16 we took her to
    <strong>Elysian Park</strong>, the park on the hills just north of downtown. At 2:51 she was at the right edge of the
    frame, with the downtown towers rising beyond the trees.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'elysian-wangcai.webp'} alt="A grassy hillside with trees and a bare tree in the middle, with the downtown Los Angeles skyline behind; a cream-colored dog stands at the right edge." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Elysian Park, with Wangcai at the right, February 16, 2025.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    A week later, on February 23, we took her up <strong>Runyon Canyon</strong>, this time at midday. At 11:51 the
    view from the trail ran all the way from Hollywood to the downtown towers.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'runyon-view-2025.webp'} alt="A wide view over Hollywood's buildings to the downtown Los Angeles skyline under a clear blue sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The view from Runyon Canyon, February 23, 2025, 11:51 am.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    At 12:27 Wangcai stopped on a slope to look down the canyon, and at 12:39 she lay down in the dirt for a rest.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'runyon-wangcai.webp'} alt="A cream-colored dog on a leash on a brushy slope, looking down a canyon toward the city in the distance." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Wangcai on the slope, 12:27 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'runyon-rest.webp'} alt="A white dog lying in the dirt beside the trail, on a leash, looking at the camera." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A rest by the trail, 12:39 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h2>Lakes and parks</h2>
  <p>
    On March 10, 2024, we spent the afternoon at the <strong>Los Angeles County Arboretum</strong> in Arcadia. We
    found a peacock under the trees at 2:29. A few minutes later there were Canada geese resting on the lawn.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'arboretum-peacock.webp'} alt="A peacock with a bright blue neck walking on wood chips under trees, its long tail trailing behind it." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A peacock at the Arboretum, 2:29 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'arboretum-geese.webp'} alt="A Canada goose resting on bright green grass in the sun." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A Canada goose on the lawn, 2:42 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    On June 29 we were in Silver Lake, where a public stairway is painted in rainbow stripes with
    a red heart at the bottom.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'silver-lake-stairs.webp'} alt="A long public stairway painted in rainbow stripes with red hearts at the bottom, between green plants and a concrete wall." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The rainbow stairs in Silver Lake, June 29, 2024, 1:03 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    On July 2, 2024, we were at <strong>Echo Park Lake</strong> at sunset. The lake's fountain was running, with the
    downtown towers lined up behind it, and the lotus beds in the foreground were a solid green. At the edge of the
    lake stands the <em>Lady of the Lake</em>, a tall stone statue of a woman with her hands raised, and at 8:01 she
    was framed against the fountain and the pale evening sky.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'echo-park-fountain.webp'} alt="A tall fountain in Echo Park Lake with the downtown Los Angeles towers behind it at dusk, and green lotus beds in the foreground." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The fountain and downtown, 8:00 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'echo-park-lady.webp'} alt="A tall stone statue of a woman with raised hands beside the lake fountain, with palm trees and towers under a pale sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Lady of the Lake, 8:01 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    <strong>Machado Lake</strong>, in Ken Malloy Harbor Regional Park, is at the other end of the city, near the
    harbor. On March 8, 2025, we walked there with Wangcai. Tall reeds line the lake, and across the water stand the
    tanks and stacks of a refinery, one of them sending up a white plume.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'machado-reeds.webp'} alt="Tall reeds in the foreground at the edge of a lake, with refinery tanks and stacks on the far shore under a blue sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Reeds and the refinery across the lake, 3:56 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'machado-lake.webp'} alt="A calm blue lake with reeds at its edge and a refinery with a white plume on the far shore." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Machado Lake, 4:02 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    In the middle of the lake, at 4:19, a log was floating, wrapped in green strands. Five minutes later
    Wangcai was standing on the paved path under the trees.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'machado-log.webp'} alt="A log floating on dark blue water, wrapped in green strands, with a single reed stem in the foreground." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A log in the lake, 4:19 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'machado-wangcai.webp'} alt="A cream-colored dog standing on a curving paved path beside a wooden post, with trees and a lawn behind." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Wangcai on the path, 4:24 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    The last outing here is from October 12, 2025: a hilltop trail in the <strong>Puente Hills</strong>, on the
    east side of the basin, with Wangcai. The grass was dry and gold, and at 5:07 the trail ran along the top
    of the hill with the city spread out below in the evening haze.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'puente-hills.webp'} alt="A cream-colored dog on a leash standing on a narrow dirt trail through dry golden grass, with a hazy city view below." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Wangcai in the Puente Hills, October 12, 2025, 5:07 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Nineteen outings over four years, none of them more than a few hours. The 2022 photos are mostly hikes; the later
    ones are mostly walks, and from 2025 on, most of them include Wangcai.
  </p>
